</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">VI-C</span> </span><span id="S6.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Forget out-of-domain data</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The OOD (Out-of-Domain) scenario is necessary to evaluate the domain generalization performance of our framework. By using the FFHQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite> dataset, which differs from the training dataset (CelebA-HQ), we test whether our method can generalize beyond the training domain and effectively unlearn a specific instance. As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T6" title="Table VI ‣ VI-C Forget out-of-domain data ‣ VI Discussion ‣ Unlearning the Unpromptable: Prompt-free Instance Unlearning in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>, the OOD case also demonstrated similar model integrity to the InD (In-Domain) case.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S6.T6.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE VI</span>: </span><span id="S6.T6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Single instance unlearning for InD and OoD data.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S6.T6.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S6.T6.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
